alty Library; the Science Museum \nLibrary and the University of York’s Borthwick Institute for Archives. All these \nhave my gratitude but some individuals call for especial mention. \n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nJony Ive by Leander Kahney: 9781591847069 | PenguinRandomHouse.com: Books (https://www.penguinrandomhouse.com/books/313546/jony-ive-by-leander-kahney/)\nciteturn16search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 12.0 years ago; Crawled: last month; \n\nImage: Jony Ive by Leander Kahney Image\n\nBest Seller\n\nImage Look Inside\n\n# Jony Ive\n\nImage: Jony Ive by Leander Kahney\n\nPaperback $17.00\n\nPublished on Oct 28, 2014 | 336 Pages\n\n[Button: Add to Cart] [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input]\n\nBuy from Other Retailers:\n\nImage: Jony Ive by Leander Kahney\n\nEbook\n\nPublished on Nov 14, 2013 | 320 Pages\n\nImage: Jony Ive by Leander Kahney\n\nAudiobook Download\n\nPublished on Nov 14, 2013 | 8 Hours 46 Minutes\n\nSee All Formats (2) +\n\nPaperback\n\n–\n\n  * [Button: Paperback ] $17.00\n\nPublished on Oct 28, 2014 | 336 Pages\n\n[Button: Add to Cart] [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input]\n\nBuy from Other Retailers:\n\nEbook\n\n+\n\nAudio\n\n+\n\n### Book Description Image\n\n“An adulating biography of Apple’s left-brained wunderkind, whose work continues to revolutionize modern technology.” —Kirkus Reviews\nIn 1997, Steve Jobs discovered a scruffy British designer toiling away at Apple’s headquarters, surrounded by hundreds of sketches and prototypes. Jony Ive’s collaboration with Jobs would produce some of the world’s most iconic technology products, including the iMac, iPod, iPad, and iPhone. Ive’s work helped reverse Apple’s long decline, overturned entire industries, and created a huge global fan base. Yet little is known about the shy, soft-spoken whiz whom Jobs referred to as his “spiritual partner.”\n\nLeander Kahney offers a detailed portrait of the English art school student with dyslexia who became the most acclaimed tech designer of his generation. Drawing on interviews with Ive’s former colleagues and Apple insiders, Kahney “takes us inside the creation of these memorable objects.” (The Wall Street Journal)\n\n[Button: See More ]\n\n### Product Details Image\n\nISBN 9781591847069\n\nPublished on Oct 28, 2014\n\nPublished by Portfolio\n\nPages 336\n\nDimensions 5-1/2 x 8-7/16\n\nAuthor\n\nLeander Kahney\n\nLeander Kahney, the bestselling author of Inside Steve’s Brain, Jony Ive and Cult of Mac, has covered Apple for more than a dozen years. He is the former news editor of Wired.com, and now the editor of CultofMac.com.\n\nLearn More about Leander Kahney\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe epic of steel | WorldCat.org (https://search.worldcat.org/title/773208732)\nciteturn16search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: 63.9 years ago; Crawled: last week; \n\nImage: Front cover image for The epic of steel\n\n# \n\nThe epic of steel\n\nAuthor:\n\nDouglas A. Fisher\n\n* * *\n\nPrint Book, English, 1963\n\nPublisher: Harper & Row, New York, 1963--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe Optical Munitions Industry in Great Britain (https://theses.gla.ac.uk/3451/1/2005SambrookPhD.pdf)\nciteturn16search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 14.6 years ago; Crawled: 14.4 years ago; \nThe Optical Munitions Industry in Great Britain  \n1888 to 1923\n\nStephen Curtis Sambrook\n\nThesis submitted for the Degree of Doctor of Philosophy\n\nUniversity of Glasgow  \nDepartment of Economic and Social History\n\nJune 2005--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nArt and Design Book List (https://qualifications.pearson.com/content/dam/pdf/GCSE/Art%20and%20Design/2016/teaching-and-learning-materials/art-and-design-book-list.pdf)\nciteturn16search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.2 years ago; \n<table id=\"e1\">\n<tr>\n<th>Section</th>\n<th>Book Title</th>\n<th>Author</th>\n<th>Format</th>\n<th>Description and Link</th>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Product Design</td>\n<td>Jony Ive: The Genius Behind Apple’s Greatest Products</td>\n<td>Leander Kahney</td>\n<td>Paperback: 336 pages</td>\n<td>An intimate look at the legendary British designer behind Apple’s most iconic products - including the Apple Watch\n\nWith the death of Steve Jobs in 2011, JONY IVE has become the most important person at Apple. Some would argue he always was.\n\nSteve Jobs discovered Ive in 1997, when he found the scruffy British designer toiling away in a studio surrounded by hundreds of sketches and prototypes. Jobs instantly realised he had found a talent who could reverse Apple’s decline, and become his ‘spiritual partner’.\n\nTheir collaboration produced iconic products including the iMac, iPod, iPad and iPhone. Designs that overturned entire industries and created the world’s most powerful brand.\n\nLink to book</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Product Design</td>\n<td>Karim20</td>\n<td>Karim Rashid</td>\n<td>Hardcover: 455 pages</td>\n<td>‘Karim 20’ is a retrospective of two decades of Karim Rashid’s design work. His designs include luxury goods, furniture, lighting, brand identity and art design.\n\nLink to book</td>\n</tr>\n</table>\n\n65\n\nGo Back to Contents--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTITLE (https://files.ehs.org.uk/wp-content/uploads/2020/11/29060829/2004-Conference-Booklet.pdf)\nciteturn16search15 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 22.7 years ago; \nNew Researchers - Session I / D\n\n# The British optical munitions industry before the Great War\n\nStephen Sambrook, University of Glasgow  \n(0104120s@student.gla.ac.uk)  \nSupervisor: Professor RG Stokes\n\n‘Optical munitions’ are optical instruments designed particularly for military and naval applications. They include rangefinders, gun sighting telescopes, submarine periscopes and prismatic binoculars, all incorporating precisely worked optical systems and intended to withstand adverse conditions not encountered by optical devices made for commercial use. By 1914 these products had become essential for all armies and navies, and their scale of employment was sufficiently large for a distinctive industry to have emerged in Great Britain.\n\nThe performance of this industry before the Great War has previously been seen as generally unfavourable. Roy and Kay MacLeod depicted it as craft-based, outdated, inefficient, and lacking capital both for investment and research.¹ According to their account, in June 1914 the domestic optical industry was so weak that it could not meet the needs of the Army and Navy. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBook Reviews, Sites, Romance, Fantasy, Fiction | Kirkus Reviews (https://www.kirkusreviews.com/book-reviews/a/douglas-a-fisher/the-epic-of-steel/)\nciteturn16search5 [wordlim: 200] Published: 63.6 years ago; Crawled: 5 months ago; \n\n  *   *   * If there is one tangible item more essential than all others to what mankind has been in recorded history and even more...\n\nREAD REVIEW\n\n# THE EPIC OF STEEL\n\nby Douglas A. Fisher ‧ RELEASE DATE: March 27, 1963\n\nIf there is one tangible item more essential than all others to what mankind has been in recorded history and even more important to what we are today and will be in any foreseeable future, certainly that item is steel. Yet, as Mr. Fisher notes in his preface, the last comprehensive book on the subject was published in 1891; and most of the great innovations in use and production have come in the past 70 years. The text is lucid, easy reading, the many tables are well-designed for reference, and the complex processes involved are explained as well as can be in clear simple language.\n\nPub Date: March 27, 1963\n\nISBN: N/A\n\nPage Count: -\n\nPublisher: Harper & Row\n\nReview Posted Online: N/A\n\nKirkus Reviews Issue: March 1, 1963\n\nCategories: NONFICTION\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDouglas A. Fisher | Open Library (https://openlibrary.org/authors/OL10276362A/Douglas_A._Fisher)\nciteturn16search6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; \n\nLast edited by ImportBot\n\nMarch 1, 2022 | History\n\n# Douglas A. Fisher\n\nImage: We need a photo of Douglas A. Fisher\n\n## 4 works Add another?\n\nMost Editions\n\n  * Preview\n\n### Steel Serves the Nation 1901-1951\n\nby Douglas A. Fisher First published in 1951\n2 editions, 1 ebook\n\nBorrow\n    * Listen\n    * Nearby Libraries\n\n  * No Preview\n\n### Steel: From the Iron Age to the Space Age\n\nby Douglas A. Fisher First published in 1967\n1 edition\n\nNearby Libraries\n\n  * No Preview\n\n### The epic of steel\n\nby Douglas A. Fisher First published in 1963\n1 edition\n\nNearby Libraries\n\n  * No Preview\n\n### Steel Serves the Nation, 1901-1959: The 50 Year Story of United States Steel\n\nby Douglas A. Fisher First published in 1951\n1 edition\n\nNearby Libraries\n\nImage: We need a photo of Douglas A. Fisher\n\n### Subjects\n\nIron industry and trade Steel industry and trade Steel, juvenile literature\n\n### ID Numbers\n\n  * OLID: OL10276362A\n\n### Links outside Open Library\n\nNo links yet. Add one?\n\nDownload catalog record: RDF / JSON--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOptics & Photonics News - How the Great War Changed the Optics Industry (https://www.optica-opn.org/home/articles/volume_27/january_2016/features/how_the_great_war_changed_the_optics_industry/)\nciteturn16search7 [wordlim: 200] Published: 10.8 years ago; Crawled: last month; \n  * J.A. Fleming and C.S. Piggot. “Frederick Eugene Wright, 1877-1953,” Biographical Memoirs, v. 29, pp. 317-359 (National Academy of Sciences, 1956).\n  * R. MacLeod and K. MacLeod. “War and economic development: Government and the optical industry in Britain,” in J.M. Winter, ed., War and Economic Development: